   208	<header class="hero">
   209	  <div class="kick">Cross-implementation AMICA · ds004505 · real EEG · bleeding-edge</div>
   210	  <h1>Chunk size is the <em>hidden variable</em></h1>
   211	  <p class="lede">On real EEG, the batching knob moves fit time up to ~30× and peak memory up to ~30×.
   212	  Every implementation is strongly chunk-sensitive; the defaults are footguns. jamica is fastest
   213	  at every chunk and leanest at full-batch.</p>
   214	  <div class="stamp"><span><b>Latest main:</b></span><span>jamica <code>df18b5e</code></span><span>scott <code>e15e158</code></span><span>pyamica <code>a8a4d7e</code></span><span>pAMICA <code>0c4da39</code></span><span>Fortran ref <code>665b577</code></span><span>· 25 subj · 64 comp · 100 iters · H100 + 8-core Xeon</span></div>
   215	</header>
   216	
   217	<section>
   218	  <h2>The correction that motivated this</h2>
   219	  <p class="sub">A naive comparison uses each library's default batching. pAMICA's default
   220	  (<code>block_size=512</code>) alone produced a ~15–47× "gap" that is a configuration artifact, not an
   221	  algorithmic one — on the same real subjects, the same pAMICA:</p>
   222	  <div class="callout">
   223	    <div class="stat bad"><div class="big">140→9.2s</div><div class="lab">pAMICA on ds004505 (GPU median) from its 512 default to full-batch — a 15× self-speedup from one number.</div></div>
   224	    <div class="stat warn"><div class="big">~30×</div><div class="lab">Range of every library across the chunk axis on real data. The default is never the optimum, and the optimum flips by device.</div></div>
   225	    <div class="stat jamica"><div class="big">1.8×</div><div class="lab">jamica's real lead over pAMICA when both run at their own optimum — not tens of ×.</div></div>
   226	  </div>
   227	  <table><thead><tr><th><code>block_size</code></th><th>Fit time</th><th>Peak VRAM</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody>{pamrows()}</tbody></table>
   228	  <p class="note">pAMICA's torch backend does not auto-tune <code>block_size</code>; reporting its
   229	  shipped default as "pAMICA performance" is what produced the headline gap.</p>
   230	</section>
   245	    scott-huberty <b>OOMs</b> — while jamica reaches its best time in ~8 GB (≈2 GB when chunked).</li>
   246	  </ul>
   247	</section>
   248	
   249	<section>
   250	  <h2>Fair comparison — each at its own optimum</h2>
   251	  <p class="sub">The authoritative ranking: real ds004505, 25-subject median, every library at
   252	  <em>its</em> best chunk on the H100.</p>
   253	  <table><thead><tr><th>Implementation</th><th>Batching</th><th>Fit time</th><th>vs jamica</th><th>Peak VRAM</th></tr></thead><tbody>{optrows()}</tbody></table>
   254	  <p class="note">jamica leads by <b>1.8×</b> over pAMICA at optima — not the tens-of-× a default
   255	  comparison implies. jamica also pays the least memory for its speed.</p>
   256	</section>
   257	
   258	<section>
   259	  <h2>CPU — fit time &amp; memory</h2>
   260	  <p class="sub">Real ds004505, 8 cores, 5 subjects (fair-share-neutral throttled rerun). Fit-time is
   261	  the <b>best-of-5 per cell</b> — a least-contended lower bound; memory is allocation-driven, so clean
   262	  regardless. ◯ = each impl's CPU optimum. Includes the Fortran amica17 reference (CPU-only).</p>
   263	  <div class="grid2"><div class="card">{c_ct}</div><div class="card">{c_cr}</div></div>
   264	  {legend(CPU_IMPLS)}
   265	  <ul class="tk" style="margin-top:20px">
   266	    <li><b>The optimum flips by device.</b> CPU optima sit at <em>small/mid</em> chunks (jamica 1024,
   267	    scott/Fortran ~4096, pyamica 16384) — the opposite of the GPU, where full-batch won. Small blocks
   268	    fit CPU cache; on the H100 they starve the device. A single recommended chunk is wrong.</li>
   269	    <li><b>jamica is fastest on CPU too</b> — best ~155 s vs scott 164, pAMICA 241, Fortran 589, pyamica 724.</li>
   270	    <li><b>Full-batch is a memory trap on CPU as well</b> (jamica 21 GB, pyamica 27 GB); the Fortran
   271	    reference is the leanest everywhere (0.7 GB).</li>
   272	    <li><b>Extremes fail:</b> pyamica@1024 exceeds the 1-hour runner timeout (~767 eager chunks/iter);
   273	    scott full-batch OOMs — both absent from the curve.</li>
   274	  </ul>
   275	  <div class="warn-box" style="margin-top:14px"><b>Why best-of-5, not the mean.</b> CPU cells ran on
   276	  shared <code>bycore</code> nodes; AMICA fits are memory-bandwidth-bound, so co-located cells starve
   277	  each other. Even the throttled rerun (job array, <code>%4</code>) leaves residual contention, and the
   278	  5 subjects differ in length — so the per-cell <em>median</em> stays non-monotonic. The <em>min</em>
   279	  across subjects recovers the clean chunk trend. Absolute CPU seconds are approximate; trust the shapes
   280	  and ordering. Full analysis + noise estimate: <code>NOTES_measurement.md</code>.</div>
   281	</section>
   282	
   283	<section>
   284	  <h2>Methodology — why the synthetic "flat curve" was a mirage</h2>
   285	  <p class="sub">An earlier synthetic microbenchmark (random data, 20 iters) showed jamica with a
   286	  suspiciously flat GPU curve. An independent panel (GPT-5.6 + Grok-4.6) and a steady-state
   287	  re-measurement traced it to a <b>JIT-compile artifact</b>, not robustness.</p>
   288	  <p>With <code>chunk_size</code> a JIT static argument, each of jamica's fused-scan cells compiled a
   289	  different program; a fresh-process 20-iter wall clock folds a ~2.5 s compile into every point, which
   290	  flattens a short run. Splitting compile from steady-state ms/iter (<code>(T₄₀−T₁₀)/30</code>) reveals
   291	  a normal <b>~19× steady-state spread</b> — jamica is <em>not</em> chunk-robust. The real 100-iteration
   292	  workload above confirms it: a steep, ordinary U-curve. What jamica genuinely has is a fused in-graph
   293	  blocked E-step (no Python-per-block penalty), memory-efficient full-batch, a correct <code>auto</code>
   294	  pick per device, and the fastest real-workload time — not knob-insensitivity.</p>
   295	  <div class="quote">"Flattest ⇒ most robust is a self-serving reading of a short, compile-contaminated
   296	  GPU sweep." — adversarial panel synthesis</div>
   297	</section>
   298	
   299	<section>
   300	  <h2>Release vs main</h2>
   301	  <p class="sub">All curves above are latest <code>main</code>. Release→main is performance-neutral on
   302	  GPU (measured identical); jamica's one perf-relevant commit (<code>2cd81e4</code>, "make CPU fits
   303	  faster and smaller") touches the CPU E-step only. Competitor <code>main</code> builds move only
   304	  through the same batching knob; bumping to main rescues no one's default (pAMICA <code>main</code> is
   305	  still 140 s at 512). The CPU curves above are the clean throttled <code>main</code> build; a
   306	  like-for-like release-vs-main CPU comparison at clean concurrency was out of scope for this pass.</p>
   307	</section>
